    "result_text": "I implemented `answer()` in `app/chat.py` and all 7 tests pass (`pytest -q`).\n\n**How it works**\n- Each attempt runs the full `iter_answer` stream and joins the text. The text is returned only if the stream finishes, so a dropped attempt's partial text is thrown away.\n- Connection failures and mid-stream drops are retried from the start, up to `ATTEMPTS` (3) attempts in total. Each failure logs a WARNING on `app.chat` in this form: `Attempt 2/3 to reach the model failed: RemoteProtocolError: peer closed connection without sending complete message body`. If all attempts fail, it raises `UpstreamUnavailable`, chained to the last error.\n- A timeout raises `UpstreamTimeout` right away with no retry.\n- The client is used exactly as you pass it in; no retry settings are changed.\n- `history` is turned into a list once at the start. Otherwise a one-shot iterator would be used up by the first attempt, and retries would silently leave out the conversation history.\n\n**One thing about openai 3.19.2 worth knowing**\nThe SDK wraps network errors in its own exceptions, both when connecting and while the answer is streaming. Their messages are just \"Connection error.\" or \"Request timed out.\", so logging those alone wouldn't tell ops anything. The log line reads the original network error (and its type) from the exception's `__cause__` instead. I also had to check for the timeout error first, because in the SDK it's a subtype of the connection error; checked the other way round, timeouts would be retried.\n\n**Tests**\nThe new tests in `tests/test_chat.py` use a real `OpenAI` client with a simulated network (`httpx2.MockTransport`, which ships with the SDK, so nothing new was installed). That means the SDK's own error handling is tested, not a stand-in for it. They cover:\n- a normal answer\n- a failed connection, then a drop mid-answer, then success: only the final text is returned, the history is sent on every attempt, and both warnings include the network error's message\n- all attempts "
